<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shatuo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Shatuo Turks</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Shatuo were a Turkic people of the Western Turkic tradition who settled on the northern frontier of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> in the 9th century. Their chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-keyong">Li Keyong</a> helped the Tang retake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huang-chao">Huang Chao</a> in 883 and was rewarded with Hedong, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a>. Shatuo rulers founded three of the Five Dynasties: the Later Tang, Later Jin and Later Han.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shatuo.jpg" alt="Shatuo" width="320" height="138" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Author: Benjamin Banayan (rugrabbit.com), photographed at the Metropolitan Museum of Art exhibit &quot;COURT AND COSMOS: The Great Age … · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Court_of_Seljuk_ruler_Tughril_III.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Zhaoli Qaghan was murdered in 832, and in 839 Zhangxin Qaghan was forced to commit suicide by the minister Qasar, who seized the throne with 20,000 <b>Shatuo</b> horsemen from Ordos.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uyghur-khaganate">Uyghur Khaganate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Khitan Liao army took Kaifeng and ended the Later Jin, the <b>Shatuo</b> general Liu Zhiyuan, governor of Taiyuan, proclaimed himself emperor.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-10">10 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Li Keyong, chief of the <b>Shatuo Turks</b>, helped the Tang drive Huang Chao out of Chang'an in 883 and was rewarded with the governorship of Hedong, centred on Taiyuan.</blockquote><a href="/state/jin-five-dynasties">Jin (Five Dynasties)</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Khitan army of the Liao dynasty took Kaifeng and ended the Later Jin (936-947) at the start of 947, Liu Zhiyuan, <b>Shatuo</b> governor of Hedong at Taiyuan, proclaimed himself emperor.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-han">Later Han</a></li><li><blockquote>Shi Jingtang, a <b>Shatuo</b> general and son-in-law of Emperor Mingzong of the Later Tang, rebelled at Taiyuan in 936.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-jin-936-947">Later Jin (936-947)</a></li><li><blockquote>The ruling house and much of its army were <b>Shatuo Turks</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-tang">Later Tang</a></li></ul></section>
